how would I add them in, what would bethe most logical way? 

Use a theme layer: define the characters once, keep your rendering logic unchanged, and substitute theme chars when building lines. Steps:

Add a Theme field for box characters (or a separate Unicode_THEME).
Replace the hardcoded ASCII pieces ("+", "-", "|", " ") in _build_static_lines with theme properties.
Keep color/RESET handling the same.
Example minimal Theme (add to your themes module or in code):

python


UNICODE_THEME = Theme(
    WALL="", RESET="",
    ENTRY="\x1b[32m", EXIT="\x1b[31m", PATH="\x1b[33m",
    # box drawing
    CORNER_TL="┌", CORNER_TR="┐", CORNER_BL="└", CORNER_BR="┘",
    H="─", V="│", T_UP="┴", T_DOWN="┬", T_LEFT="┤", T_RIGHT="├", CROSS="┼",
)
Adjust _build_static_lines to use those symbols. Example replacements (conceptual):

Top border: instead of f"{self.theme.WALL}+" and "---+", build using CORNER_TL / CORNER_TR / H:
start with self.theme.CORNER_TL
for each cell add self.theme.H*3 and appropriate junction (use CROSS or T_DOWN depending on neighbor)
Vertical walls: use self.theme.V instead of "|" and horizontal segments use self.theme.H instead of "-".
For empty gaps use spaces the same width.
Conservative concrete change pattern (simpler, keeps existing layout):

Replace "+" with a theme.JUNCTION (e.g., "┼"), "-" with theme.H, and "|" with theme.V.
Keep the same spacing and cell-width logic.
Small code sketch swapping tokens in your current implementation:

python


corner = getattr(self.theme, "JUNCTION", "+")
h = getattr(self.theme, "H", "-")
v = getattr(self.theme, "V", "|")

top = f"{v}{corner}"
for x in range(width):
    if self.maze[0][x] & NORTH:
        top += h*3 + corner
    else:
        top += "   " + corner

middle = f"{v}{v}"
# and where you had f" {content} {self.theme.WALL}|{self.theme.RESET}"
# use f" {content} {v}{self.theme.RESET}"
Notes:

Unicode box-drawing needs a font/terminal that supports it (most modern ones do).
Color escape sequences don't affect column width; avoid adding visible extra chars in theme strings.
You can provide both ASCII and UNICODE themes and let users pick.
If you want, I can produce a ready-to-drop-in Theme object plus the exact modified build_static_lines code. Which would you prefer?